= 26 05 00 Common Work Results for Electrical

+ Work conforms to CSA C22.1, Canadian Electrical Code, Part I, as adopted in Alberta.
+ Materials are new and CSA certified. Material descriptions on the materials schedule, drawing E-000, are the Owner's standard descriptions.
+ Feeder F1: three \#3 copper conductors plus a \#8 copper bond in 35 mm EMT, as shown on E-001.
+ Test every circuit for continuity and insulation resistance before energizing. Provide a typed directory in panel LP-1.

= 26 05 19 Low-Voltage Electrical Power Conductors

+ Copper conductors, type THHN/THWN-2, 90 °C, 600 V.
+ Branch circuits: \#12 AWG minimum, three conductors per circuit (line, neutral, bond).

= 26 05 33 Raceways and Boxes

+ Interior raceways: electrical metallic tubing (EMT) with steel set-screw fittings, 21 mm minimum.
+ Boxes: steel, sized to code. Fittings, boxes, supports, and connectors are incidental to the raceway and devices.

= 26 22 13 Low-Voltage Transformers

+ Transformer T1: dry-type, 75 kVA, 600 V delta primary, 120/208 V wye secondary, 150 °C rise, floor mounted on vibration isolation pads.
+ Secondary conductors to LP-1 and the bonding connection to the building ground bus are part of the transformer installation.

= 26 24 16 Panelboards

+ Panelboard LP-1: 42 circuits, 225 A copper bus, 225 A main breaker, 120/208 V, 3 phase, 4 wire, surface mounted, 10 kA interrupting rating.
+ Branch breakers: 20 A, 1 pole, bolt-on, for every circuit in use and every spare shown on E-002.

= 26 27 26 Wiring Devices

+ Duplex receptacles: 15 A, 125 V, CSA 5-15R, specification grade, white, with box and device plate.
+ Switches: single-pole, 15 A, 120 V, specification grade, white.

= 26 51 00 Interior Lighting

+ 2x4 LED troffer: recessed in the T-bar ceiling, 40 W, 4000 K, minimum 4,400 lumens, on and off control.

= 26 52 00 Emergency Lighting

+ Exit signs: LED, green running man pictogram to the National Building Code of Canada, 5 W, universal mounting.
+ Emergency battery units: 12 V, with two heads, 30 minute runtime, self-diagnostic.
